1. (Currently Amended)    A device system for measuring and assessing anesthesia of an animal subject in real-time from based on electroencephalography (EEG) measurements of the animal subject during application of anesthesia, the device system comprising:a signal pre-processor comprising at least two filters in different filtering frequency a first filter with a first filtering frequency, a second filter with a second filtering frequency, and a down-sampler to pre-process sequentially an incoming measured EEG signal from the EEG measurements of the animal subject;wherein the pre-processing comprises:for removing unwanted signals and noise from the incoming measured EEG signal stream of the system to using the first filter and the second filter to generate a filtered incoming measured EEG signal; and generate an incoming down-sampling the filtered incoming measured EEG signal by the down-sampler to generate a down-sampled filtered incoming measured EEG signal for subsequent epoch generation;

an epoch generator for generating configured to generate an epoch signal containing epochs of 1-second epoch duration each and 10% overlapping of consecutive epochs;wherein the epoch generator comprises comprising a two-input multiplexer, an address generator, and a memory[[,]];

wherein the two-input multiplexer receiving is configured to:receive the incoming down-sampled filtered incoming measured EEG signal from the signal pre-processor, a feedback of the epoch signal,

and also a selector input from the address generator; andgenerate a multiplexer output from the down-sampler output signal or the feedback of the epoch signal based on the selector input;



to feed an input signal stream to the memory after a counter value of a counter at a relatively higher frequency from the address generator reaches 500;

wherein the address generator comprises:a slow counter configured to generate write addresses, and count from a slow counter start count to a slow counter end count in repeating slow count cycles; and

a fast counter configured to generate read addresses, and count from a fast counter start count to a fast counter end count in repeating fast count cycles that starts after each of the slow count cycles;having two counters at different frequencies and generating two counter signals with write address and read address, respectively, to be fed to the memory, and

also wherein the slow counter is further configured to repeat each of the slow count cycles after each of the fast count cycles;

wherein the fast counter is further configured to repeat each of the fast count cycles after each of the slow count cycles;

wherein the address generator is further configured to:generate the selector input to the multiplexer to generate the multiplexer output from the down-sampler output signal during each of the slow count cycles and from the feedback of the epoch signal during each of the fast count cycles;

a control signal to be fed directly for subsequent feature extraction;

wherein the memory is configured to:receiving receive the input signal stream from the two-input multiplexer output, and two counter signals with the write address addresses,and the read address addresses[[,]];

store the received multiplexer output according to the received write addresses; and

output a memory output data according to the received read addresses;

wherein the epoch signal being the memory output data;



respectively, from the address generator and then generating the epoch signal containing the 1-second and 10% overlapping epochs for subsequent feature extraction;

a feature extractor comprising configured to:a derivative calculator and a variance calculator, the derivative calculator receiving the epoch signal containing 1-second and 10% overlapping epochs from the memory of the epoch generator and calculating a mean of accumulated squared-differences among different epochs; the variance calculator receiving absolute value of each of the epochs accumulated and determining a mean of the accumulated epochs, obtaining a deviation of an epoch by subtracting the mean from one of the absolute values of the epoch, calculating a squared deviation followed by determining square root of an average squared deviation for subsequent classification;

receive the epoch signal from the epoch generator;

extract a derivative feature ofthe received epoch signal by computing a mean ofaccumulated sqiuared-differences among the epochs in the received epoch signal; and

extract a variance feature ofthe received epoch signal by computing a square root ofan average squared-deviation ofthe epochs in the received epoch signal;

a classifier configured to:comprising two cascaded units for expanding features extracted by the feature extractor by double the number of the features followed by feature mapping to set a classification boundary, and subsequently using an output of the feature mapping to obtain a sigmoid function as a decision boundary in order for subsequent prediction;perform a feature mapping on the derivative feature and the variancefetr;and

set a decision boundary from output ofthe feature mapping to generate a classifier output, the classifier output being either that the animal subject being awake or anesthetized or that the animal subject being awake or anesthetized; and

a predictor comprising a predictor circuit configured to:for accumulating accumulate a plurality of the classifier's outputs[[,]];



constraining compute a decision value effrom the accumulated classifier's outputs between 0 and 1,;

and determining determine a level of anesthesia of the animal subject i-n terms of from the constrained decision value based on the animal subject's real- time electroencephalogram;and

predict the animal subject's anesthesia condition from a plurality of determined levels of anesthesia of the animal subject.



2. (Currently Amended)    The system of claim 1, wherein [[a]] the first filter of the signal pre-processor is configured with the first has a filtering frequency to compensate the for compensating a power-line interference of the incoming measured EEG signal from the electroencephalography of the animal subject.



3. (Currently Amended)    The system of claim 1, wherein [[a]] the second filter of the signal pre-processor is configured with the second has a filtering frequency being comparable to an average frequency of a wide electroencephalography frequency region in a range of 0-250 Hz of the incoming measured EEG signal from the animal subject;

wherein the average frequency of the wide electroencephalography band of the incoming measured EEG signal is in the range of 0-250 Hz.



4. (Currently Amended)    The system of claim 1, wherein the incoming measured EEG signal is down sampled at least ten times after being subject subjected to the second filter using the down-sampler.



5. (Currently Amended)    The system of claim 1, wherein the epoch generator creates a sliding window to divide each electroencephalography channel into short-overlapped epochs. It is optimally designed to consume lower resources and the smallest possible size of memory unit (RAM).

6. (Currently Amended)    The system of claim 1, wherein the derivative calculator of the feature extractor computing of a mean of accumulated squared-differences among different epochs in the received epoch signal comprises:a multiplier to square the Docket No. P2096US00
5 differences among different epochs before calculating the mean of the accumulated squared-differences, and further comprises an accumulator and divisor for calculating the mean computing a difference between two consecutive epochs of the epochs in the received epoch signal and a square of the difference to obtain a squared difference;

repeating the computation to obtain a squared difference for each pair of consecutive epochs in the received epoch signal;

accumulating all of the squared differences obtained; and

determining the mean of the accumulated squared differences.

7. (Currently Amended)
The system of claim 1, wherein the variance calculator computing of a square root of an average squared-deviation of the epochs in the received epoch signal comprises:a multiplier, an accumulator and a divisor circuit to consecutively calculate the average squared deviation; the variance calculator further comprises a register to store the average squared deviation for being fed to the classifier subsequently obtaining an absolute epoch value of each of the epochs in the received epoch signal;

accumulating all of the absolute epoch values obtained;

determining a mean of the accumulated absolute epoch values;

computing a difference between each of the absolute epoch values and the mean of the accumulated absolute epoch values and a square of the difference to obtain a squared deviation;

computing an average of all of squared deviations for all of the absolute epoch values; and

computing a square root of the average of all of squared deviations.



8. (Canceled)

9. (Currently Amended)
The system of claim 1, wherein the predictor is configured to accumulate the plurality of the determined levels level of anesthesia in terms of percentage and constrain an output value as a decision score between 0 and 1 with respect Docket No. P2096US00
6 to the percentage of the anesthesia of the animal subject, wherein a 100% anesthesia corresponds to the decision score of 1, while a 0% anesthesia corresponds to the decision score of 0.



10. (Currently Amended) A method for determining depth of anesthesia (DOA) of an animal subject in real-time from a transient behavior of an electroencephalography (EEG) measurements using machine learning techniques thereof, the method comprising:detecting and measuring an EEG signal of the animal subject using an EEGelectrode;

recording electroencephalographic (EEG) signal of the animal subject and filtering thereof within a relatively lower frequency band the EEG signal to remove unwanted signals and noise;

down-sampling the filtered EEG signal to at least ten times for reducing data size and accelerating subsequent processing without losing essential features of the EEG signal for subsequent classification;

generating epochs from the down-sampled EEG signals signal an epoch signal containing epochs of 1-second epoch duration each and 10% overlapping of consecutive epochs comprising segmenting each EEG channel with a shortened signal time and overlapping one EEG signal with a preceding EEG signal thereof;

extracting two features from each epoch selected from derivative and variance thereof;

extracting a derivative feature of the epoch signal by computing a mean of accumulated squared-differences among the epochs in the epoch signal;

extracting a variance feature of the epoch signal by computing a square root of an average squared-deviation of the epochs in the epoch signal;mapping the derivative feature and the variance features feature to determine a classification boundary, followed by using an output of the mapping to determine a sigmoid function as a decision boundary between awake and anesthetized state comprising:if the mapping output value is lower than 0.5 or 50%,the epoch is classified as a classifier output is set to that the animal subject being awake during a time duration of the epochs; otherwise, the epoch is classified as the classifier Docket No. P2096US00
7 output is set to that the animal subject being anesthetized during a time duration of the epochs;and

accumulating a plurality of the classifier output value outputs of consecutive awake and anesthetized epochs, respectively, and constraining each of the output value between 0 and 1 in order to predict a likelihood of a successive awake or anesthetized epoch generate a prediction output of a likelihood of the animal subject being awake or anesthetized in a successive epoch.



11. (New)    The method of claim 10, further comprising:increasing a prediction probability of the animal subject being anesthetized if the prediction output is that the animal subject being anesthetized, or decreasing the probability of the animal subject being anesthetized if the prediction output is that the animal subject being awake; and

adjusting a successive prediction output based on the prediction probability.



12. (New)    The method of claim 10, further comprising:generating a confident prediction output of the animal subject being awake if the prediction output is at or below 25% likelihood of the animal subject being anesthetized;

and generating a confident prediction output of the animal subject being anesthetized if the prediction output is at or above 75% likelihood of the animal subject being anesthetized.



13. (New)    The method of claim 10, wherein the filtering of the EEG signal to remove unwanted signals and noise comprising:filtering the EEG signal at a filtering frequency being comparable to an average frequency of a wide electroencephalography frequency band of the incoming measured EEG signal;

wherein the average frequency of the wide electroencephalography band of the incoming measured EEG signal is in the range of 0-250 Hz.





14. (New)    The method of claim 10, wherein the computing of a mean of accumulated squared-differences among different epochs in the epoch signal comprises:computing a difference between two consecutive epochs of the epochs in the epoch signal and a square of the difference to obtain a squared difference;

repeating the computation to obtain a squared difference for each pair of consecutive epochs in the epoch signal;

accumulating all of the squared differences obtained; and

determining the mean of the accumulated squared differences.



15. (New)    The method of claim 10, wherein the computing of a square root of an average squared-deviation of the epochs in the epoch signal comprises:obtaining an absolute epoch value of each of the epochs in the epoch signal;

accumulating all of the absolute epoch values obtained;

determining a mean of the accumulated absolute epoch values;

computing a difference between each of the absolute epoch values and the mean of the accumulated absolute epoch values and a square of the difference to obtain a squared deviation;

computing an average of all of squared deviations for all of the absolute epoch values; and

computing a square root of the average of all of squared deviations.
